2. Automating it with GitHub Actions
Tauri provides an official GitHub Action called tauri-apps/tauri-action that handles the heavy lifting.

To set it up:

Create the Workflow File: Inside your project repository, create a new folder path: .github/workflows/ and add a file named release.yml.

Add the Configuration: Paste the following configuration into release.yml. This script tells GitHub to spin up a Windows server, install Rust and Node.js, build your application, and automatically create a GitHub release containing the installer.

[YAML]

name: 'publish'

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - release # This action will trigger whenever you push code to a branch named "release"

jobs:
  publish-tauri:
    permissions:
      contents: write
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        platform: [windows-latest] # We only need Windows for this app

    runs-on: ${{ matrix.platform }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      # Install Node.js
      - name: setup node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: lts/*

      # Install Rust
      - name: install Rust stable
        uses: dtolnay/rust-toolchain@stable

      # Install frontend dependencies
      - name: install frontend dependencies
        run: npm install

      # Use the official Tauri action to build and release
      - uses: tauri-apps/tauri-action@v0
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        with:
          tagName: app-v__VERSION__ # The action automatically replaces this with the version in your tauri.conf.json
          releaseName: 'War Thunder Assistant v__VERSION__'
          releaseBody: 'See the assets to download the installer.'
          releaseDraft: true
          prerelease: false


3. How to Trigger the Build
Push your code to GitHub on your main branch.

When you are ready to create an installer, create a new branch named release and push it to GitHub.

The action will automatically trigger. You can watch its progress in the "Actions" tab of your GitHub repository.

Once finished, a new draft release will appear in your repository's "Releases" section. The final .msi and .exe installer files will be attached to it, ready for anyone to download!